1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,120

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:05,870
Thanks so much.

3
00:00:05,870 --> 00:00:06,680
Great to be here.

4
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:10,770
And Happy Halloween--
belated Halloween, everyone.

5
00:00:10,770 --> 00:00:14,190
So I think the talk is going
to be split into two sections.

6
00:00:14,190 --> 00:00:18,020
So I'll start by spending like
10 minutes, 15 minutes chatting

7
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:20,785
about transformers in general.

8
00:00:20,785 --> 00:00:22,910
But I'm assuming most of
you are familiar with them

9
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:26,510
and we can move on to NPTs,
which Jannik and Neil will

10
00:00:26,510 --> 00:00:28,760
be presenting.

11
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:35,750
So let's see, I'm going to try
to fly through the transformer

12
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:40,160
overview and maybe spend
a little bit extra time

13
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:42,110
on the history of
transformers and maybe just

14
00:00:42,110 --> 00:00:43,790
tell the story a little bit.

15
00:00:43,790 --> 00:00:47,880
I think that might
be more interesting.

16
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:51,680
So just in terms of the
transformer architecture,

17
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:54,740
the two kind of things that it
introduced for the first time

18
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:58,130
were multi-headed attention
and self-attention.

19
00:00:58,130 --> 00:01:01,400
And then, it combined those with
fast autoregressive decoding.

20
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:04,190
So before the transformer,
pretty much everyone

21
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:06,410
was using LSTMs.

22
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:09,675
And LSTMs with attention.

23
00:01:09,675 --> 00:01:11,300
But I'll try to get
into the difference

24
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:15,770
of self-attention,
multi-headed attention.

25
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:19,550
So originally, you
have two sequences

26
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:23,570
and you would have a attention
module, which would attend

27
00:01:23,570 --> 00:01:25,980
from the source to the target.

28
00:01:25,980 --> 00:01:29,240
And so, each token or each
word in the source sequence

29
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:33,170
would get associated with
a soft approximation of one

30
00:01:33,170 --> 00:01:37,340
element in the target sequence.

31
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:40,220
And so, you'd end up
with something like this.

32
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:42,020
But with self-attention,
we did away

33
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:43,730
with the two separate sequences.

34
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:46,080
We make them both the same.

35
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:49,160
And so, you're relating each
element within the sequence

36
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:52,910
to another element
in the sequence.

37
00:01:52,910 --> 00:01:57,140
And so, the idea
here is that you're

38
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:59,360
learning a relationship
of the words

39
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:01,040
within a sentence
to the other words.

40
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:04,670
So you can imagine something
like an adjective, which

41
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:05,950
is being applied to a noun.

42
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:09,020
And so you want to relate that
adjective-- like the blue ball,

43
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:12,860
you want to relate blue
as referring to ball.

44
00:02:12,860 --> 00:02:15,380
So we're learning patterns
within the sequence--

45
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:16,505
intra-sequence patterns.

46
00:02:16,505 --> 00:02:19,910

47
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:22,340
Sorry, I gave this
talk in Kenya.

48
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:25,640
So I'm using Swahili here.

49
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:28,220
But with multi-head
attention, the idea

50
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:30,590
is you have each
word represented

51
00:02:30,590 --> 00:02:33,600
by an embedding, which is
in the depth dimension here.

52
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:36,020
And then, you have
your sentence of words.

53
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:39,690
You split that up into a
bunch of different groups.

54
00:02:39,690 --> 00:02:42,920
So here, I've chopped it
depth-wise into four groups.

55
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:47,330
You apply attention to each one
of these groups independently.

56
00:02:47,330 --> 00:02:49,820
And then, when you
get the result back,

57
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:53,900
you concatenate them together
and you're back to your model

58
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:56,610
dimension representation.

59
00:02:56,610 --> 00:03:01,415
So what this lets you do is, if
each attention-- each attention

60
00:03:01,415 --> 00:03:04,590
head can now focus on
learning one pattern.

61
00:03:04,590 --> 00:03:08,420
So maybe attention head one
is learning the relationship

62
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:11,150
of adjectives to nouns.

63
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:14,430
And the second attention head
can learn something different.

64
00:03:14,430 --> 00:03:18,380
So this lets us learn
a hierarchy or a list

65
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:20,960
of different relationships.

66
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:24,380
OK, so that was self-attention.

67
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:27,725
The other piece is fast
autoregressive decoding.

68
00:03:27,725 --> 00:03:30,500

69
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,590
And do I really want
to go into this?

70
00:03:33,590 --> 00:03:34,220
OK, I will.

71
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:36,290
So the important
thing about this

72
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:40,920
is that if you're doing normal
autoregressive decoding, what

73
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:42,990
you do is you generate
your first token.

74
00:03:42,990 --> 00:03:44,780
And now, conditioned
on that first token,

75
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:45,780
you generate the second.

76
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:48,113
And conditioned on the first
two, you generate the third

77
00:03:48,113 --> 00:03:49,080
and so on and so forth.

78
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:50,400
That's super slow, right?

79
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:53,950
It's a loop applying this
thing again and again.

80
00:03:53,950 --> 00:03:56,400
And so, what we
can do instead is

81
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:58,950
we make an assumption
in the code

82
00:03:58,950 --> 00:04:02,850
that our model always
generates the right thing.

83
00:04:02,850 --> 00:04:06,960
And we generate-- and then,
we generate a prediction

84
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:08,640
only one token ahead.

85
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:13,050
And so, the way that
this looks is you--

86
00:04:13,050 --> 00:04:17,430
OK, so y hat here--

87
00:04:17,430 --> 00:04:19,709

88
00:04:19,709 --> 00:04:20,209
sorry.

89
00:04:20,209 --> 00:04:21,350
One second.

90
00:04:21,350 --> 00:04:22,230
Input to output.

91
00:04:22,230 --> 00:04:24,380
So you have your
outputs, which are y.

92
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:29,240
You have your targets,
which are y hat.

93
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:32,450
And what you do is you
feed in those gold targets

94
00:04:32,450 --> 00:04:35,580
so that you don't need
to actually do this loop.

95
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:37,580
So instead of assuming--
instead of having

96
00:04:37,580 --> 00:04:39,710
to generate the first
token, feed it back

97
00:04:39,710 --> 00:04:42,500
into your architecture,
generate a second token,

98
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:45,830
you feed in the entire
target sequence.

99
00:04:45,830 --> 00:04:48,470
And you just pretend that you
generate all the right tokens

100
00:04:48,470 --> 00:04:50,240
up to position k.

101
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:52,760
And then, you predict
the k plus first.

102
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:55,472
And you compute
your loss on that.

103
00:04:55,472 --> 00:04:56,930
And so in reality,
your model might

104
00:04:56,930 --> 00:05:00,380
have generated at the
beginning of training junk.

105
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:02,750
But you're getting a
loss as if your model

106
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:05,870
had seen all the correct
tokens and is now just

107
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:08,030
predicting the next one.

108
00:05:08,030 --> 00:05:10,700
This is a little
bit subtle, but it's

109
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:13,730
hugely impactful for training
speed because all of this

110
00:05:13,730 --> 00:05:15,410
can be done in parallel.

111
00:05:15,410 --> 00:05:18,935
And so, it's actually what
makes transformers so scalable.

112
00:05:18,935 --> 00:05:22,090

113
00:05:22,090 --> 00:05:24,250
OK, so in order to
do this successfully,

114
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:29,560
if you were just feeding in
all of the correct tokens

115
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:32,050
naively, what would
happen is your model

116
00:05:32,050 --> 00:05:38,230
would just be able to look
forward in time and cheat.

117
00:05:38,230 --> 00:05:41,320
So you've put in all of your
true targets, the things

118
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,552
that you're trying to get
your model to predict.

119
00:05:43,552 --> 00:05:45,760
And so, if that's what you're
computing your loss on,

120
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:47,410
you could just look
forward in time

121
00:05:47,410 --> 00:05:49,330
and say, OK, I'm just
going to grab that

122
00:05:49,330 --> 00:05:51,340
and would get 0 error
trivially, right?

123
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:53,990
Because you've given it
all the right answers.

124
00:05:53,990 --> 00:05:56,170
So what we have to do
inside the architecture

125
00:05:56,170 --> 00:05:59,980
is we need to actually prevent
the attention mechanism

126
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:02,890
from being able to look at
tokens that it shouldn't

127
00:06:02,890 --> 00:06:05,770
have been able to see already.

128
00:06:05,770 --> 00:06:10,150
So the way that this
looks is you create a mask

129
00:06:10,150 --> 00:06:12,970
on your attention.

130
00:06:12,970 --> 00:06:15,160
And so, sorry,
this is the example

131
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:17,830
of doing a trivial attention.

132
00:06:17,830 --> 00:06:22,090
If you don't mask your attention
properly, what it's going to do

133
00:06:22,090 --> 00:06:24,160
is it's just going to
look into the future,

134
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:27,670
just grab the token that
you're telling it to predict,

135
00:06:27,670 --> 00:06:28,390
and copy it over.

136
00:06:28,390 --> 00:06:29,890
And so, it learns
something trivial.

137
00:06:29,890 --> 00:06:31,598
Something it doesn't
actually generalize.

138
00:06:31,598 --> 00:06:33,730
And so, what we
do is we actually

139
00:06:33,730 --> 00:06:35,740
prevent it from attending
to those tokens.

140
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:38,680
We prevent it from
attending into the future.

141
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:41,350
For each position in
the source sequence,

142
00:06:41,350 --> 00:06:45,970
we block out everything that
it shouldn't be able to see.

143
00:06:45,970 --> 00:06:47,410
Everything into the future.

144
00:06:47,410 --> 00:06:49,150
And then, as we move
down, we gradually

145
00:06:49,150 --> 00:06:54,110
unblock so it can start
to see into the past.

146
00:06:54,110 --> 00:06:55,630
So those are kind of the two--

147
00:06:55,630 --> 00:07:00,640
the three major components
of transformers.

148
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:05,380
The self-attention, the
multi-head attention, and then,

149
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:07,120
deploying this gold targets--

150
00:07:07,120 --> 00:07:09,385
decoding this fast
autoregressive decoding.

151
00:07:09,385 --> 00:07:12,220

152
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:14,170
In terms of the
story, which might be

153
00:07:14,170 --> 00:07:18,600
a little bit more interesting.

154
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:21,260
So transformers--
I was an intern

155
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:26,210
with Lukasz Kaiser at
Google back in 2017.

156
00:07:26,210 --> 00:07:29,480
And I was sitting next
to Noam and she was

157
00:07:29,480 --> 00:07:33,110
a couple of seats down from us.

158
00:07:33,110 --> 00:07:36,140
And what's really incredible
is that, essentially,

159
00:07:36,140 --> 00:07:39,290
this entire project came
together in like three months

160
00:07:39,290 --> 00:07:40,500
and it was done.

161
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:42,800
So I showed up at Google.

162
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:47,420
Noam had been working on
autoregressive models.

163
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:50,030
Same thing with Ashish
and Jakab and Nikki.

164
00:07:50,030 --> 00:07:54,080
And they had just been kind
of like exploring the space,

165
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:56,000
figuring it out.

166
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,530
And Lukasz and I,
at the same time,

167
00:07:57,530 --> 00:07:59,363
we had been working on
this framework called

168
00:07:59,363 --> 00:08:03,140
Tensor2Tensor,
which was explicitly

169
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:08,810
made for multimodal learning,
autoregressive learning.

170
00:08:08,810 --> 00:08:14,190
And Lukasz is kind of
a master of keeping

171
00:08:14,190 --> 00:08:16,290
track of everything that's
happening in the field

172
00:08:16,290 --> 00:08:17,490
and adopting it.

173
00:08:17,490 --> 00:08:20,010
And so, within
Tensor2Tensor there

174
00:08:20,010 --> 00:08:26,620
were these emerging little
things that maybe one paper had

175
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:29,320
been written about and
people were interested in,

176
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:31,570
like LayerNorm but
it hadn't actually

177
00:08:31,570 --> 00:08:37,330
taken off yet, the warm up in
the learning rate schedule.

178
00:08:37,330 --> 00:08:40,480
All of these little
pieces were just default--

179
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:45,910
like on by default. And so,
when Noam and Ashish and Nikki

180
00:08:45,910 --> 00:08:50,470
and Jakab came over and
adopted Tensor2Tensor,

181
00:08:50,470 --> 00:08:54,610
all of these things were
just on by default. And so,

182
00:08:54,610 --> 00:08:58,173
a lot of people, when they
look at the transformer paper,

183
00:08:58,173 --> 00:08:59,590
it just seems like
there's so many

184
00:08:59,590 --> 00:09:02,350
arbitrary little
things thrown in.

185
00:09:02,350 --> 00:09:06,490
And now, in present day, these
have become standard for a lot

186
00:09:06,490 --> 00:09:09,070
of different training
algorithms, like the learning

187
00:09:09,070 --> 00:09:12,160
rate warm up, the
way that we did

188
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:14,860
initialization,
all of these pieces

189
00:09:14,860 --> 00:09:16,450
have just become the norm.

190
00:09:16,450 --> 00:09:21,040
But back then, they had kind
of just been introduced.

191
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:25,710
And so, we spent a lot of
time running ablations trying

192
00:09:25,710 --> 00:09:27,930
to figure out which were
the necessary pieces

193
00:09:27,930 --> 00:09:29,280
and what made it work.

194
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:33,030
And if any of you have actually
tried training transformers

195
00:09:33,030 --> 00:09:35,160
and tried pulling
out the learning rate

196
00:09:35,160 --> 00:09:39,360
warm up or changing any
of these little pieces,

197
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:42,570
you'll see that it really
does break down optimization.

198
00:09:42,570 --> 00:09:46,005
It actually really
does hurt performance.

199
00:09:46,005 --> 00:09:48,630
For instance, like removing the
LayerNorms, that type of thing.

200
00:09:48,630 --> 00:09:51,260

201
00:09:51,260 --> 00:09:54,820
I always thought it
was kind of funny how

202
00:09:54,820 --> 00:09:57,578
all of these random additions
that Lukasz had just

203
00:09:57,578 --> 00:09:59,620
thrown in because he was
playing around with them

204
00:09:59,620 --> 00:10:05,810
turned out to be crucial, and
they were just on by default.

205
00:10:05,810 --> 00:10:07,780
So anyway, it was
like three months.

206
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:12,880
I remember it all really
started coming together

207
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:15,475
towards the end, like just
before the NeurIPS deadline.

208
00:10:15,475 --> 00:10:18,000

209
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,700
And I can still remember sitting
in the micro kitchen and Ashish

210
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:22,200
telling me-- like
it was just like--

211
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:23,790
I was a little intern.

212
00:10:23,790 --> 00:10:26,190
Telling me like, this is
going to be such a big deal.

213
00:10:26,190 --> 00:10:27,870
And I was like, yeah, sure, OK.

214
00:10:27,870 --> 00:10:29,850
I have no idea what's happening.

215
00:10:29,850 --> 00:10:33,210
I just showed up.

216
00:10:33,210 --> 00:10:36,060
And he was like, no, dude,
like this actually matters.

217
00:10:36,060 --> 00:10:38,650
We bumped up BLEU three points.

218
00:10:38,650 --> 00:10:40,515
And I was like,
sick, great, anyway.

219
00:10:40,515 --> 00:10:44,480

220
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:46,370
And then, I can
remember on the night

221
00:10:46,370 --> 00:10:49,340
of the deadline for NeurIPS.

222
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:51,800
It was like 2:00 AM.

223
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:56,360
Ashish was the only
one left at the office

224
00:10:56,360 --> 00:10:58,700
and we were still like
moving around figures

225
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:00,140
and adjusting things.

226
00:11:00,140 --> 00:11:03,080
And then, I went to bed
but Ashish stayed up.

227
00:11:03,080 --> 00:11:07,543
And I slept in like this
tiny little phone booth.

228
00:11:07,543 --> 00:11:09,710
And then, for the other
paper that I was submitting,

229
00:11:09,710 --> 00:11:11,330
I forgot to press Submit.

230
00:11:11,330 --> 00:11:15,650
But luckily, some lady opened
