286
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:54,470
This is more or less a
step through of the paper,

287
00:13:54,470 --> 00:13:57,980
but maybe, with a little bit of
extra insight here and there.

288
00:13:57,980 --> 00:14:01,790

289
00:14:01,790 --> 00:14:05,130
All right, as promised, the
motivation, a brief summary.

290
00:14:05,130 --> 00:14:08,930
So we'll start by thinking about
something that we don't often

291
00:14:08,930 --> 00:14:12,950
think about, and that is that
from CNNs to transformers,

292
00:14:12,950 --> 00:14:15,200
most of supervised
deep learning relies

293
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:17,610
on parametric prediction.

294
00:14:17,610 --> 00:14:20,960
So what that means is that we
have some set of training data.

295
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:25,130
And we want to learn to predict
the outcomes y from the inputs

296
00:14:25,130 --> 00:14:25,830
x.

297
00:14:25,830 --> 00:14:29,600
And first, we set up some model
with tunable parameters theta.

298
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:31,850
Then, we optimized
these parameters

299
00:14:31,850 --> 00:14:35,090
to maximize predictive
likelihoods on a training set

300
00:14:35,090 --> 00:14:39,200
or, equivalently, we
minimize the loss.

301
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:42,200
And then, after training,
we have this optimized set

302
00:14:42,200 --> 00:14:43,700
of parameters theta.

303
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:46,700
And then, at test time, we
just put these into the model

304
00:14:46,700 --> 00:14:50,540
and use these parameters to
predict on novel test data.

305
00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:53,810
And so, crucially here,
our prediction at test time

306
00:14:53,810 --> 00:14:56,240
only depends on these
parameters, right?

307
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:58,400
It's parametric.

308
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:00,320
Also, that means that
given these parameters,

309
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:03,230
the prediction is entirely
independent of the training

310
00:15:03,230 --> 00:15:04,590
data.

311
00:15:04,590 --> 00:15:08,862
And so, why would we want
to do parametric prediction?

312
00:15:08,862 --> 00:15:10,070
Well, it's really convenient.

313
00:15:10,070 --> 00:15:12,070
Because all that we've
learned from the training

314
00:15:12,070 --> 00:15:14,810
data can be summarized
in the parameters.

315
00:15:14,810 --> 00:15:16,700
And so, at prediction
time, we only

316
00:15:16,700 --> 00:15:18,470
need these final
parameters and we do not

317
00:15:18,470 --> 00:15:20,053
need to store the
training data, which

318
00:15:20,053 --> 00:15:22,140
might be really, really large.

319
00:15:22,140 --> 00:15:24,380
On the other hand,
we usually have

320
00:15:24,380 --> 00:15:26,930
models that already
predict for a bunch of data

321
00:15:26,930 --> 00:15:28,220
in parallel, right?

322
00:15:28,220 --> 00:15:30,770
Think of mini batching
and modern architectures.

323
00:15:30,770 --> 00:15:32,840
And actually, things
like batch norm

324
00:15:32,840 --> 00:15:36,050
already make these
data interact.

325
00:15:36,050 --> 00:15:38,540
And so, our thinking
here was that if we've

326
00:15:38,540 --> 00:15:40,760
got all of this data
in parallel anyways,

327
00:15:40,760 --> 00:15:43,260
there's no reason not
to make use of it.

328
00:15:43,260 --> 00:15:48,500
And so, more a bit grander, we
challenge parametric prediction

329
00:15:48,500 --> 00:15:51,390
as the dominant paradigm
in deep learning.

330
00:15:51,390 --> 00:15:55,130
And so, we want to give models
the additional flexibility

331
00:15:55,130 --> 00:15:57,710
of using the training
data directly

332
00:15:57,710 --> 00:15:59,780
when making predictions.

333
00:15:59,780 --> 00:16:02,060
And so, a bit more
concretely, we

334
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:05,360
introduced the non-parametric
transformer architecture.

335
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:08,060
And this is going to be
a general deep learning

336
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:10,220
architecture, meaning
we can apply it

337
00:16:10,220 --> 00:16:12,800
to a variety of scenarios.

338
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:17,730
NPTs will take the entire data
set as input whenever possible.

339
00:16:17,730 --> 00:16:21,680
And NPTs then, crucially, learn
to predict from interactions

340
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:24,080
between data points.

341
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:27,590
And to achieve this, we use
multi-head self-attention

342
00:16:27,590 --> 00:16:30,770
that, as Aidan has
introduced us to,

343
00:16:30,770 --> 00:16:33,770
has just really established
itself as the general purpose

344
00:16:33,770 --> 00:16:36,060
layer for reasoning.

345
00:16:36,060 --> 00:16:39,630
We also take another thing
from the NLP community

346
00:16:39,630 --> 00:16:43,110
and we use a stochastic
masking mechanism.

347
00:16:43,110 --> 00:16:45,990
And we use that to tell NPTs
where to predict, and also

348
00:16:45,990 --> 00:16:49,240
to regularize the
learning task of it.

349
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:51,210
And lastly, of course,
we hope to convince you

350
00:16:51,210 --> 00:16:53,280
that this ends up
working really well

351
00:16:53,280 --> 00:16:56,280
and that this simple
idea of learning

352
00:16:56,280 --> 00:16:59,340
to predict from the other
datapoints of the input,

353
00:16:59,340 --> 00:17:01,023
from the training
points of the input,

354
00:17:01,023 --> 00:17:02,190
and supporting them as well.

355
00:17:02,190 --> 00:17:05,140

356
00:17:05,140 --> 00:17:05,640
Sorry.

357
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:09,530
And so, very briefly summarizing
what we've heard already.

358
00:17:09,530 --> 00:17:13,740
A, we input into NPTs
the entire data set.

359
00:17:13,740 --> 00:17:17,030
And then, B, let's say, for
the purpose of this slide here,

360
00:17:17,030 --> 00:17:19,880
we only care about predicting
the orange question

361
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:22,220
mark in that green row.

362
00:17:22,220 --> 00:17:26,060
And then, we can compare NPTs
to parametric prediction, right?

363
00:17:26,060 --> 00:17:28,280
So a classical
deep learning model

364
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:30,560
would predict this
target value only

365
00:17:30,560 --> 00:17:33,410
from the features of
that single green input.

366
00:17:33,410 --> 00:17:35,630
To do that, it would use
the parameters theta.

367
00:17:35,630 --> 00:17:37,490
Those would depend
on whatever training

368
00:17:37,490 --> 00:17:38,820
data we've seen and so on.

369
00:17:38,820 --> 00:17:42,590
But at test time, we only look
at that single row for which

370
00:17:42,590 --> 00:17:44,450
we care about the prediction.

371
00:17:44,450 --> 00:17:48,140
In contrast, NPTs predict
an explicit dependence

372
00:17:48,140 --> 00:17:50,180
on all samples in the input.

373
00:17:50,180 --> 00:17:53,180
They can look beyond that
single gradation of interest.

374
00:17:53,180 --> 00:17:55,340
And look at all other
[INAUDIBLE] that are there

375
00:17:55,340 --> 00:17:57,630
and consider their
values for prediction.

376
00:17:57,630 --> 00:18:00,710
So this presents an
entirely different way

377
00:18:00,710 --> 00:18:04,100
of thinking about how we
learn predictive mechanisms.

378
00:18:04,100 --> 00:18:06,890
And somebody on Twitter
called this k-NN 2.0,

379
00:18:06,890 --> 00:18:09,260
which we would have not
