380
00:18:09,260 --> 00:18:10,790
But maybe it is
kind of a nice way

381
00:18:10,790 --> 00:18:15,620
of thinking about how
NPTs can learn to predict.

382
00:18:15,620 --> 00:18:18,890
So of course, non-parametric
models are a thing already.

383
00:18:18,890 --> 00:18:20,390
We didn't invent them at all.

384
00:18:20,390 --> 00:18:23,780
And I define them
here as prediction

385
00:18:23,780 --> 00:18:26,750
in explicit dependence on
the training data, which

386
00:18:26,750 --> 00:18:28,760
is certainly what NPTs do.

387
00:18:28,760 --> 00:18:31,100
Classical examples,
like Gaussian processes,

388
00:18:31,100 --> 00:18:33,080
k nearest neighbor,
kernel methods,

389
00:18:33,080 --> 00:18:35,390
those might be familiar to you.

390
00:18:35,390 --> 00:18:37,670
And there exists, also,
efforts to combine

391
00:18:37,670 --> 00:18:40,340
