multiple data sets.

506
00:23:25,450 --> 00:23:29,560

507
00:23:29,560 --> 00:23:30,910
Cool.

508
00:23:30,910 --> 00:23:31,630
OK, awesome.

509
00:23:31,630 --> 00:23:33,790
If there's no other
questions, I'll

510
00:23:33,790 --> 00:23:36,100
dive a bit more into
the architecture.

511
00:23:36,100 --> 00:23:39,250

512
00:23:39,250 --> 00:23:40,150
Awesome.

513
00:23:40,150 --> 00:23:43,423
So there's three key
components to NPTs.

514
00:23:43,423 --> 00:23:45,340
I'm going to first state
them at a high level.

515
00:23:45,340 --> 00:23:48,590
And then, we'll go through
each of them in more detail.

516
00:23:48,590 --> 00:23:51,190
So first of all, we take
the entire data set.

517
00:23:51,190 --> 00:23:53,420
All data points as input.

518
00:23:53,420 --> 00:23:55,150
So for example, at
test time, the model

519
00:23:55,150 --> 00:23:58,720
is going to take as input
both training and test data.

520
00:23:58,720 --> 00:24:03,870
And we approximate this with
mini batches for large data.

521
00:24:03,870 --> 00:24:06,400
We apply self-attention
between data points.

522
00:24:06,400 --> 00:24:09,690
So for example, at test
time, we model relationships

523
00:24:09,690 --> 00:24:12,120
amongst training points,
amongst test points,

524
00:24:12,120 --> 00:24:15,040
and between the two sets.

525
00:24:15,040 --> 00:24:17,490
And then, finally, we have
this masking-based training

526
00:24:17,490 --> 00:24:18,570
objective.

527
00:24:18,570 --> 00:24:20,580
It's a BERT-like
stochastic masking.

528
00:24:20,580 --> 00:24:22,740
And the key point
is that we actually

529
00:24:22,740 --> 00:24:25,690
use it on both features as
well as on training targets.

530
00:24:25,690 --> 00:24:27,750
And we'll get into
why that leads

531
00:24:27,750 --> 00:24:32,010
to an interesting
predictive mechanism later.

532
00:24:32,010 --> 00:24:35,810
So to start with, this
idea of data sets as input.

533
00:24:35,810 --> 00:24:38,930
There's two things that
compose the input to NPT.

534
00:24:38,930 --> 00:24:40,790
It's a full data set
in the form of a matrix

535
00:24:40,790 --> 00:24:44,780
X and a masking matrix
M. And so, Jannik

536
00:24:44,780 --> 00:24:47,240
has described this data
set matrix a little bit.

537
00:24:47,240 --> 00:24:49,610
We basically have
data points as rows.

538
00:24:49,610 --> 00:24:50,900
The columns are attributes.

539
00:24:50,900 --> 00:24:53,960
And each attribute shares
some kind of semantic meaning

540
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:56,173
among all of its data points.

541
00:24:56,173 --> 00:24:58,340
So say, for example, you're
just doing single target

542
00:24:58,340 --> 00:25:00,500
classification or regression.

543
00:25:00,500 --> 00:25:02,460
The last column
would be the target,

544
00:25:02,460 --> 00:25:04,830
and the rest of the matrix
would be input features.

545
00:25:04,830 --> 00:25:08,530
So for example, the
pixels of an image.

546
00:25:08,530 --> 00:25:10,390
We also have a masking matrix.

547
00:25:10,390 --> 00:25:13,710
So let's say, we're thinking
about mask language modeling.

548
00:25:13,710 --> 00:25:15,210
The mask tokens
will just tell us

549
00:25:15,210 --> 00:25:17,190
where we're going
to conceal words

550
00:25:17,190 --> 00:25:19,800
and where we're going
to backpropagate a loss.

551
00:25:19,800 --> 00:25:21,630
We do a similar type
of thing here where

552
00:25:21,630 --> 00:25:23,760
we use this binary
mask matrix to specify

553
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:26,150
which entries are masks.

554
00:25:26,150 --> 00:25:29,690
And the goal is to
predict masked values

555
00:25:29,690 --> 00:25:32,030
from observed values.

556
00:25:32,030 --> 00:25:35,390
I see that there was a
question about handling inputs

557
00:25:35,390 --> 00:25:37,940
with different lengths.

558
00:25:37,940 --> 00:25:41,180
In the data sets
we've considered,

559
00:25:41,180 --> 00:25:42,920
we'll get into it in
the results section,

560
00:25:42,920 --> 00:25:45,380
but it's mostly been sort
of tabular and image data

561
00:25:45,380 --> 00:25:48,350
where the lengths for each of
the data points is the same,

562
00:25:48,350 --> 00:25:50,000
but it would work
just like padding.

563
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:53,052
That would be a reasonable
way to go about that.

564
00:25:53,052 --> 00:25:55,010
And there's also kind of
an interesting-- yeah.

565
00:25:55,010 --> 00:25:56,360
Go for it, Jannik.

566
00:25:56,360 --> 00:25:57,420
Just to add to that.

567
00:25:57,420 --> 00:26:02,270
I'm not sure if length refers
to columns or to rows, right?

568
00:26:02,270 --> 00:26:04,670
Rows we don't care
about how many rows.

569
00:26:04,670 --> 00:26:07,670
Length, padding or something
would be an option.

570
00:26:07,670 --> 00:26:10,250
Yeah, my question was
about columns exactly.

571
00:26:10,250 --> 00:26:11,940
So that makes sense.

572
00:26:11,940 --> 00:26:13,685
Thanks.

573
00:26:13,685 --> 00:26:16,060
Yeah, I mean, that goes along
with the whole meta earning

574
00:26:16,060 --> 00:26:17,710
discussion is, I
think, if we wanted

575
00:26:17,710 --> 00:26:20,560
to adapt two data sets that
have a different number of data

576
00:26:20,560 --> 00:26:23,440
points per data set, we can
take advantage of the fact

577
00:26:23,440 --> 00:26:25,840
that self-attention
is OK with that.

578
00:26:25,840 --> 00:26:28,320

579
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:28,820
Cool.

580
00:26:28,820 --> 00:26:31,420

581
00:26:31,420 --> 00:26:34,120
So continuing on.

582
00:26:34,120 --> 00:26:37,390
Left to discuss
here is, basically,

583
00:26:37,390 --> 00:26:39,033
how we do the embedding.

584
00:26:39,033 --> 00:26:40,450
So to put this
more explicitly, we

585
00:26:40,450 --> 00:26:44,290
have this data matrix that has
n data points, it's called X.

586
00:26:44,290 --> 00:26:46,450
And it also has d attributes.

587
00:26:46,450 --> 00:26:48,607
And we have this
binary mask matrix M.

588
00:26:48,607 --> 00:26:49,690
We're going to stack them.

589
00:26:49,690 --> 00:26:52,660
And then, we're going to
do a linear embedding.

590
00:26:52,660 --> 00:26:55,840
So specifically, we're doing
the same linear embedding

591
00:26:55,840 --> 00:26:57,580
independently for
each data point.

592
00:26:57,580 --> 00:27:00,520
We're learning a different
embedding for each attribute.

593
00:27:00,520 --> 00:27:03,503
We have a positional encoding
on the index of the attributes

594
00:27:03,503 --> 00:27:05,170
because we don't
really care about, say,

595
00:27:05,170 --> 00:27:06,808
being equivariant
over the columns.

596
00:27:06,808 --> 00:27:08,350
If it's tabular
data, you, of course,

597
00:27:08,350 --> 00:27:10,558
want to treat all these kind
of heterogeneous columns

598
00:27:10,558 --> 00:27:11,360
differently.

599
00:27:11,360 --> 00:27:14,140
And then, finally, we have an
encoding on the type of column,

600
00:27:14,140 --> 00:27:17,720
so whether or not it's
continuous or categorical.

601
00:27:17,720 --> 00:27:21,140
And that ends up giving us this
input data set representation

602
00:27:21,140 --> 00:27:23,780
that is dimensions n by d by e.

603
00:27:23,780 --> 00:27:26,900

604
00:27:26,900 --> 00:27:29,480
The second key component
of NPTs is the attention

605
00:27:29,480 --> 00:27:31,840
between data points.

606
00:27:31,840 --> 00:27:35,440
So to do that, we first take
this representation we have

607
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:39,910
and flatten to an n by d
times e representation.

608
00:27:39,910 --> 00:27:42,130
So basically, we're treating
each of these d times

609
00:27:42,130 --> 00:27:46,030
e size rows as if it's
a token representation.

610
00:27:46,030 --> 00:27:48,700
We're actually going to just
accomplish this operation using

611
00:27:48,700 --> 00:27:50,437
multi and self-attention.

612
00:27:50,437 --> 00:27:51,520
We've reviewed this a lot.

613
00:27:51,520 --> 00:27:54,562
But the nice thing is that we
know from language modeling,

614
00:27:54,562 --> 00:27:56,020
if we stack this
multiple times, we

615
00:27:56,020 --> 00:27:57,850
can model these higher
order dependencies.

616
00:27:57,850 --> 00:27:59,870
And here, they're
between data points.

617
00:27:59,870 --> 00:28:02,260
And that's really the key
draw of this architecture.

618
00:28:02,260 --> 00:28:05,740
There's been other instances of
people using attention verse--

619
00:28:05,740 --> 00:28:06,980
for similar sorts of things.

620
00:28:06,980 --> 00:28:09,700
So for example, like
attentive neural processes.

621
00:28:09,700 --> 00:28:13,270
A lot of times they've sort
of use just a single layer

622
00:28:13,270 --> 00:28:15,892
as kind of a
representational lookup.

623
00:28:15,892 --> 00:28:17,350
And we believe that
this, actually,

624
00:28:17,350 --> 00:28:18,760
ends up limiting expressivity.

625
00:28:18,760 --> 00:28:20,302
And that by stacking
this many times,

626
00:28:20,302 --> 00:28:23,080
you can learn more complex
relationships between the data

627
00:28:23,080 --> 00:28:24,382
points.

628
00:28:24,382 --> 00:28:25,840
Neil, you also have
some questions.

629
00:28:25,840 --> 00:28:28,590

630
00:28:28,590 --> 00:28:29,850
You can go ahead first.

631
00:28:29,850 --> 00:28:31,000
Oh, cool.

632
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:31,500
Thanks.

633
00:28:31,500 --> 00:28:34,140
I have a question about how
you guys do the embedding.

634
00:28:34,140 --> 00:28:37,620
Is it always like-- or do
we use convolutional filters

635
00:28:37,620 --> 00:28:40,020
or linear layers?

636
00:28:40,020 --> 00:28:42,060
What is the type of
embedding that you guys use?

637
00:28:42,060 --> 00:28:45,480
Yeah, so I'm attempting
to go back to the slide.

638
00:28:45,480 --> 00:28:47,670
I think it's not very
happy with me right now.

639
00:28:47,670 --> 00:28:52,600
But yeah, so for tabular data,
we did just linear embeddings,

640
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:53,100
actually.

641
00:28:53,100 --> 00:28:58,168
So we could get into, I guess,
details of featurization

642
00:28:58,168 --> 00:28:59,460
for categorical and continuous.

643
00:28:59,460 --> 00:29:01,620
But it's literally like,
say, for categorical, you

644
00:29:01,620 --> 00:29:02,610
do a one hot encoding.

645
00:29:02,610 --> 00:29:04,710
And then, you learn
this embedding that

646
00:29:04,710 --> 00:29:07,080
is specific to that attribute.

647
00:29:07,080 --> 00:29:08,790
And then, for
numerical, I believe,

648
00:29:08,790 --> 00:29:10,800
we were just
standard normalizing.

649
00:29:10,800 --> 00:29:13,140
For the image
data, we did end up

650
00:29:13,140 --> 00:29:17,880
using a ResNet 18
encoder for CIFAR-10.

651
00:29:17,880 --> 00:29:18,977
However, I think that--

652
00:29:18,977 --> 00:29:21,060
I mean, we'll discuss that
a bit later in results.

653
00:29:21,060 --> 00:29:25,053
But that embedding
is a bit arbitrary.

654
00:29:25,053 --> 00:29:26,220
You can sort of do whatever.

655
00:29:26,220 --> 00:29:28,262
The key part of the
architecture is the attention

656
00:29:28,262 --> 00:29:29,800
between data points.

657
00:29:29,800 --> 00:29:32,270
In terms of how you actually
want to embed each attribute,

658
00:29:32,270 --> 00:29:35,490
it's kind of up to you.

659
00:29:35,490 --> 00:29:35,990
Thanks.

660
00:29:35,990 --> 00:29:39,350

661
00:29:39,350 --> 00:29:42,170
I think [AUDIO OUT]
another question.

662
00:29:42,170 --> 00:29:43,550
Same question as Victor's.

663
00:29:43,550 --> 00:29:46,260

664
00:29:46,260 --> 00:29:48,580
Awesome.

665
00:29:48,580 --> 00:29:49,570
Cool.

666
00:29:49,570 --> 00:29:53,200
So here we have attention
between data points done.

667
00:29:53,200 --> 00:29:56,840
So we can also do this
attention between attributes.

668
00:29:56,840 --> 00:30:01,450
So we reshape back to this
n by d by e representation.

669
00:30:01,450 --> 00:30:04,420
And then, we can just apply
self-attention independently

670
00:30:04,420 --> 00:30:05,465
to each row.

671
00:30:05,465 --> 00:30:07,090
In other words, to
a single data point.

672
00:30:07,090 --> 00:30:10,613
And the intuition for why
we would do this nested type

673
00:30:10,613 --> 00:30:13,030
idea where we switch between
attention between data points

674
00:30:13,030 --> 00:30:14,488
and attention
between attributes is

675
00:30:14,488 --> 00:30:17,380
just we're trying to learn
better per data point

676
00:30:17,380 --> 00:30:21,380
representations for the between
data point interactions.

677
00:30:21,380 --> 00:30:23,230
This is literally just
normal self-attention

678
00:30:23,230 --> 00:30:25,330
as you'd see in language
modeling or image

679
00:30:25,330 --> 00:30:26,600
classification.

680
00:30:26,600 --> 00:30:30,950
The attributes are
the tokens here.

681
00:30:30,950 --> 00:30:34,070
And finally, we just
rinse and repeat.

682
00:30:34,070 --> 00:30:36,628
So what are we actually
getting out of this?

683
00:30:36,628 --> 00:30:38,920
To summarize, we're learning
higher order relationships

684
00:30:38,920 --> 00:30:40,480
between data points.

685
00:30:40,480 --> 00:30:43,600
We're learning transformations
of individual data points.

686
00:30:43,600 --> 00:30:47,380
And then, importantly, NPT is
equivariant to a permutation

687
00:30:47,380 --> 00:30:49,030
of the data points.

688
00:30:49,030 --> 00:30:50,830
This basically just
reflects the intuition

689
00:30:50,830 --> 00:30:53,653
that the learned relationships
between the data points

690
00:30:53,653 --> 00:30:55,570
should not depend on the
ordering in which you

691
00:30:55,570 --> 00:30:58,030
receive them or in which
you observe your data set.

692
00:30:58,030 --> 00:31:01,150

693
00:31:01,150 --> 00:31:04,750
The third key component of NPT
is a masking-based training

694
00:31:04,750 --> 00:31:06,340
objective.

695
00:31:06,340 --> 00:31:08,710
So recall that what
we're trying to do

696
00:31:08,710 --> 00:31:10,810
is we're trying to
predict missing entries

697
00:31:10,810 --> 00:31:12,220
from observed entries.

698
00:31:12,220 --> 00:31:16,190
And those mass values can
be both features or targets.

699
00:31:16,190 --> 00:31:18,850
So again, the classic use, say,
of masked language modeling

700
00:31:18,850 --> 00:31:20,260
is to do
self-supervised learning

701
00:31:20,260 --> 00:31:22,150
on a sequence of
tokens, which you

702
00:31:22,150 --> 00:31:25,840
could think of as just having
features in our setting.

703
00:31:25,840 --> 00:31:28,180
Ours is a bit
different in that we

704
00:31:28,180 --> 00:31:30,910
do stochastic feature masking
to mask feature values

705
00:31:30,910 --> 00:31:32,740
with a probability
p sub feature.

706
00:31:32,740 --> 00:31:36,970
And then, we also do this
masking of training targets

707
00:31:36,970 --> 00:31:39,440
with this probability
p sub target.

708
00:31:39,440 --> 00:31:42,250
So if we write out the
training objective,

709
00:31:42,250 --> 00:31:45,610
we are just taking a weighted
sum of the negative log

710
00:31:45,610 --> 00:31:48,430
likelihood lost from targets
as well as from features.

711
00:31:48,430 --> 00:31:50,110
And of course, at
test time, we're

712
00:31:50,110 --> 00:31:53,800
only going to mask and compute
a loss over the targets of test

713
00:31:53,800 --> 00:31:55,100
points.

714
00:31:55,100 --> 00:31:58,090
So to break this down a
bit further and point out

715
00:31:58,090 --> 00:32:00,238
some of the cool
parts of it here,

716
00:32:00,238 --> 00:32:02,530
the thing that's highlighted
right now on the far right

717
00:32:02,530 --> 00:32:05,230
is the term relating
to the features.

718
00:32:05,230 --> 00:32:07,240
It's the feature masking.

719
00:32:07,240 --> 00:32:11,528
Basically, we find that this
has a nice regularizing effect.

720
00:32:11,528 --> 00:32:13,570
More or less, the model
can now predict anywhere.

721
00:32:13,570 --> 00:32:15,862
It makes the task a bit harder
and introduces some more

722
00:32:15,862 --> 00:32:16,420
supervision.

723
00:32:16,420 --> 00:32:19,150
And we found in an ablation for
the tabular data sets that it

724
00:32:19,150 --> 00:32:21,613
helped for 8 of 10 of those.

725
00:32:21,613 --> 00:32:23,030
And then, there's
this other term,

726
00:32:23,030 --> 00:32:24,460
which is kind of interesting.

727
00:32:24,460 --> 00:32:27,280
It's the stochastic
target masking.

728
00:32:27,280 --> 00:32:29,950
And the idea is
that you're actually

729
00:32:29,950 --> 00:32:32,650
going to have some
training targets unmasked

730
00:32:32,650 --> 00:32:37,030
to the model at input
at training time, which

731
00:32:37,030 --> 00:32:39,700
means that the NPT
can learn to predict

732
00:32:39,700 --> 00:32:42,460
the masked targets of
certain training data points

733
00:32:42,460 --> 00:32:45,280
using the targets of
other training data points

734
00:32:45,280 --> 00:32:47,500
as well as all of the
training features.

735
00:32:47,500 --> 00:32:49,060
And so, that means
you don't actually

736
00:32:49,060 --> 00:32:52,298
need to memorize a mapping
between training inputs

737
00:32:52,298 --> 00:32:53,590
and outputs in your parameters.

738
00:32:53,590 --> 00:32:56,140
You can, instead, devote the
representational capacity

739
00:32:56,140 --> 00:32:59,230
of the model to learn functions
that use other training

740
00:32:59,230 --> 00:33:01,322
features and targets as input.

741
00:33:01,322 --> 00:33:03,530
So this is kind of getting
into the idea of this sort

742
00:33:03,530 --> 00:33:05,980
of learn k-NN idea.

743
00:33:05,980 --> 00:33:10,300
Obviously, we can learn more
complex relational lookups

744
00:33:10,300 --> 00:33:11,800
and those sorts of
things from this.

745
00:33:11,800 --> 00:33:15,610
But you can imagine
one such case being--

746
00:33:15,610 --> 00:33:17,770
we have a bunch of test
data points coming in

747
00:33:17,770 --> 00:33:19,510
and we're going to
look at their features

748
00:33:19,510 --> 00:33:22,930
and use that to assign them
to clusters of training data

749
00:33:22,930 --> 00:33:23,532
points.

750
00:33:23,532 --> 00:33:25,240
And then, our prediction
for those points

751
00:33:25,240 --> 00:33:27,970
is just going to be an
interpolation of the training

752
00:33:27,970 --> 00:33:29,710
targets in that
respective cluster.

753
00:33:29,710 --> 00:33:32,350
That's an example of
something that this mechanism

754
00:33:32,350 --> 00:33:33,910
lets NPTs learn.

755
00:33:33,910 --> 00:33:37,150

756
00:33:37,150 --> 00:33:40,095
All right, so if
there's any questions,

757
00:33:40,095 --> 00:33:40,970
we can take them now.

758
00:33:40,970 --> 00:33:44,928
Otherwise, happy to take them
in the discussion or something.

759
00:33:44,928 --> 00:33:48,350

760
00:33:48,350 --> 00:33:52,360
All right, so let's discuss--

761
00:33:52,360 --> 00:33:53,790
Yeah, go for it.

