1264
00:54:49,440 --> 00:54:52,140
So I want you to focus on A now.

1265
00:54:52,140 --> 00:54:54,390
So we take one of
the tabular data

1266
00:54:54,390 --> 00:54:57,123
sets that we've used previously,
specifically, the protein data

1267
00:54:57,123 --> 00:54:58,540
set, but it doesn't
really matter.

1268
00:54:58,540 --> 00:55:00,810
What matters is that it's
a regression data set.

1269
00:55:00,810 --> 00:55:03,720
And so, now, what we do is we--

1270
00:55:03,720 --> 00:55:06,030
the top half here is
the original data set.

1271
00:55:06,030 --> 00:55:09,900
But the bottom half is a
copy of the original data

1272
00:55:09,900 --> 00:55:14,960
set where we have unveiled
the true target value.

1273
00:55:14,960 --> 00:55:17,170
And so, now, NPTs
could learn to use

1274
00:55:17,170 --> 00:55:19,710
attention between data
points to achieve arbitrarily

1275
00:55:19,710 --> 00:55:20,790
good performance.

1276
00:55:20,790 --> 00:55:23,520
They could learn to
look up the target

1277
00:55:23,520 --> 00:55:25,500
values in these
matching duplicate rows.

1278
00:55:25,500 --> 00:55:30,905
And then, paste them back into
that masked out target value.

1279
00:55:30,905 --> 00:55:32,280
Then, at test
time, of course, we

1280
00:55:32,280 --> 00:55:37,200
put in a novel test data input
where this mechanism is also

1281
00:55:37,200 --> 00:55:39,210
possible just to make
sure that it hasn't

1282
00:55:39,210 --> 00:55:41,430
learned to memorize anything.

1283
00:55:41,430 --> 00:55:43,740
But it has, actually, learned
this correct relational

1284
00:55:43,740 --> 00:55:45,310
mechanism.

1285
00:55:45,310 --> 00:55:47,880
And so, what we see
is that, indeed, NPTs

1286
00:55:47,880 --> 00:55:50,100
do successfully learn
to perform this lookup.

1287
00:55:50,100 --> 00:55:52,620
So what I'm visualizing
here is attention maps.

1288
00:55:52,620 --> 00:55:55,170
And they very clearly
show that, let's say,

1289
00:55:55,170 --> 00:55:57,390
when predicting for
this green row here,

1290
00:55:57,390 --> 00:55:59,460
this first green row,
what NPTs look at

1291
00:55:59,460 --> 00:56:03,550
is exactly only that
other green row here.

1292
00:56:03,550 --> 00:56:07,000
And so, this is really nice.

1293
00:56:07,000 --> 00:56:12,090
We can further look at the
kind of the Pearson correlation

1294
00:56:12,090 --> 00:56:14,910
between what NPTs should
predict and what they actually

1295
00:56:14,910 --> 00:56:16,030
do predict.

1296
00:56:16,030 --> 00:56:17,890
And so, this is 99.9%.

1297
00:56:17,890 --> 00:56:19,380
This is much better
than anything

1298
00:56:19,380 --> 00:56:22,120
you could achieve with
parametric prediction.

1299
00:56:22,120 --> 00:56:24,030
And so, it seems that
NPTs here can actually

1300
00:56:24,030 --> 00:56:25,500
discover this mechanism.

1301
00:56:25,500 --> 00:56:28,200
And discover, here, I feel
like is the right word.

1302
00:56:28,200 --> 00:56:31,710
Because NPTs could
have, as we've seen,

1303
00:56:31,710 --> 00:56:34,710
just also continue to predict
in parametric fashion,

1304
00:56:34,710 --> 00:56:38,130
right, from each
row independently.

1305
00:56:38,130 --> 00:56:40,380
This is really kind
of showing to us

1306
00:56:40,380 --> 00:56:43,560
that there is this bias
in the model to learn

1307
00:56:43,560 --> 00:56:45,120
to predict from other rows.

1308
00:56:45,120 --> 00:56:48,480
And of course, that is also
very attractive in this setting

1309
00:56:48,480 --> 00:56:51,390
because it allows you to
achieve arbitrary low loss

1310
00:56:51,390 --> 00:56:55,560
in this setting, or as low
as you can optimize for it.

1311
00:56:55,560 --> 00:56:58,810
And so, we kind of
take that to mean

1312
00:56:58,810 --> 00:57:01,780
that our gradient-based
discovery

1313
00:57:01,780 --> 00:57:05,630
non-parametric philosophy
seems to make some sense.

1314
00:57:05,630 --> 00:57:09,220
And so, we can take this a bit
further by performing somewhat

1315
00:57:09,220 --> 00:57:11,950
of an interventional
experiment that investigates

1316
00:57:11,950 --> 00:57:15,700
the extent to which NPTs
have actually learned

1317
00:57:15,700 --> 00:57:18,760
a robust causal mechanism
that's underlying

1318
00:57:18,760 --> 00:57:20,650
this semi-synthetic data set.

1319
00:57:20,650 --> 00:57:29,670
And so, just appending this
extra column of test data.

1320
00:57:29,670 --> 00:57:30,970
That's already kind of cool.

1321
00:57:30,970 --> 00:57:32,950
But I think we can
take it a bit further

1322
00:57:32,950 --> 00:57:36,790
and actually study if this
generalizes beyond the data

1323
00:57:36,790 --> 00:57:38,770
that we see in the
training set or beyond data

1324
00:57:38,770 --> 00:57:40,790
coming from this
specific distribution.

1325
00:57:40,790 --> 00:57:42,940
And so, what we now
do is we intervene

1326
00:57:42,940 --> 00:57:46,750
on individual duplicate
data points at test time

1327
00:57:46,750 --> 00:57:49,520
by varying their target value.

1328
00:57:49,520 --> 00:57:52,960
So now, we only care about the
prediction in a specific row.

1329
00:57:52,960 --> 00:57:54,430
We do this across all rows.

1330
00:57:54,430 --> 00:57:56,860
But at each time, we just
care about a single row.

1331
00:57:56,860 --> 00:58:00,280
What we do is we change
the target value here.

1332
00:58:00,280 --> 00:58:03,230
What we're hoping to see is
that the NPT just adjusts

1333
00:58:03,230 --> 00:58:04,480
the prediction as well, right?

1334
00:58:04,480 --> 00:58:06,940
There's a very simple
intervention experiment for us

1335
00:58:06,940 --> 00:58:09,850
to test if NPTs have actually
learned this mechanism.

1336
00:58:09,850 --> 00:58:12,280
And to some extent, it
also tests robustness.

1337
00:58:12,280 --> 00:58:15,070
Because now, we're
associating target values

1338
00:58:15,070 --> 00:58:17,830
with features that are not part
of the training distribution

1339
00:58:17,830 --> 00:58:19,160
here.

1340
00:58:19,160 --> 00:58:25,300
And so, what we see is that as
we adjust these values here,

1341
00:58:25,300 --> 00:58:27,850
this is the duplicate value.

1342
00:58:27,850 --> 00:58:29,740
And then, we here see
that the target value--

1343
00:58:29,740 --> 00:58:32,215
as we adjust them, we can
see that the correlation

1344
00:58:32,215 --> 00:58:33,460
stays really, really good.

1345
00:58:33,460 --> 00:58:35,980
It's not quite 99.9%,
like on average.

1346
00:58:35,980 --> 00:58:38,500
We're now at 99.6%.

1347
00:58:38,500 --> 00:58:40,390
But it's still very, very good.

1348
00:58:40,390 --> 00:58:44,920
And at this point, you might
be slightly annoyed with me

1349
00:58:44,920 --> 00:58:48,372
because standard
non-parametric models can also

1350
00:58:48,372 --> 00:58:49,330
solve this task, right?

1351
00:58:49,330 --> 00:58:52,330
But this is a task that I could
solve by nearest neighbors.

1352
00:58:52,330 --> 00:58:55,592
Sure, maybe, I would have to
change the input format a bit

1353
00:58:55,592 --> 00:58:57,550
because this is kind of
like in a batch setting

1354
00:58:57,550 --> 00:58:58,630
and I can just use masks.

1355
00:58:58,630 --> 00:59:01,780
But most generally,
nearest neighbor can also--

1356
00:59:01,780 --> 00:59:07,410
it also looks up different input
points based on their features.

1357
00:59:07,410 --> 00:59:09,160
Nearest neighbor doesn't
learn to do this.

1358
00:59:09,160 --> 00:59:11,243
I still think it's cool
that we need to learn this

1359
00:59:11,243 --> 00:59:14,650
because it does
require a decent amount

1360
00:59:14,650 --> 00:59:17,410
of computational sequences
that we have to learn,

1361
00:59:17,410 --> 00:59:19,510
like match all the features,
look up target value,

1362
00:59:19,510 --> 00:59:21,140
copy it back, and so on.

1363
00:59:21,140 --> 00:59:25,960
But it is, in fact, very easy
for us to complicate this task

1364
00:59:25,960 --> 00:59:28,390
to a degree such
that, essentially,

1365
00:59:28,390 --> 00:59:33,140
no other model that we know
of can solve this very easily.

1366
00:59:33,140 --> 00:59:34,990
And so, a really
simple thing to do

1367
00:59:34,990 --> 00:59:41,030
is just to add a plus 1 to
all of the duplicate values.

1368
00:59:41,030 --> 00:59:43,630
So now, our nearest
neighbor would look up

1369
00:59:43,630 --> 00:59:46,810
the right row, of course,
but it would always

1370
00:59:46,810 --> 00:59:49,010
predict the wrong target
with a plus 1 on it.

1371
00:59:49,010 --> 00:59:52,480
And in fact, many of the
models that we're aware of,

1372
00:59:52,480 --> 00:59:55,480
they're not modeling
the joint distribution

1373
00:59:55,480 --> 00:59:57,160
over features and targets.

1374
00:59:57,160 --> 00:59:59,710
What they're modeling is
the conditional distribution

1375
00:59:59,710 --> 01:00:03,440
of the targets given
the input features.

1376
01:00:03,440 --> 01:00:05,090
So they also cannot do this.

1377
01:00:05,090 --> 01:00:08,560
And so, for us, it's
really no problem at all.

1378
01:00:08,560 --> 01:00:11,860
NPTs will just learn to subtract
another one and no problems.

1379
01:00:11,860 --> 01:00:15,040
And sure, this is also still
a very synthetic setting.

1380
01:00:15,040 --> 01:00:18,070
But I do think--

1381
01:00:18,070 --> 01:00:20,410
I mean, I challenge you
to come up with something

1382
01:00:20,410 --> 01:00:23,890
that NPTs can't solve but
the other models can solve.

1383
01:00:23,890 --> 01:00:27,340
I think this-- in general,
this masking mechanism

1384
01:00:27,340 --> 01:00:29,820
and the non-parametricity
of the approach

1385
01:00:29,820 --> 01:00:33,340
is really nice in general and
leads to lots of nice behavior

1386
01:00:33,340 --> 01:00:35,060
in a variety of settings.

1387
01:00:35,060 --> 01:00:37,180
And so, with that,
I think we can

1388
01:00:37,180 --> 01:00:42,350
go to the conclusions, which
Neil is going to give you.

1389
01:00:42,350 --> 01:00:43,640
Yeah, I think--

1390
01:00:43,640 --> 01:00:46,100
I mean, we can cut out
the main part here.

1391
01:00:46,100 --> 01:00:48,320
I'll just fast forward.

1392
01:00:48,320 --> 01:00:50,130
Just look at them.

1393
01:00:50,130 --> 01:00:51,830
Yeah, I was going
to say, I think

1394
01:00:51,830 --> 01:00:54,050
you got you all get the gist.

1395
01:00:54,050 --> 01:00:56,390
NPTs take the entire
data set as input

1396
01:00:56,390 --> 01:00:58,940
and they use self-attention
to model complex relationships

1397
01:00:58,940 --> 01:01:01,010
between data points.

1398
01:01:01,010 --> 01:01:04,520
They do well in
experiments on tabular data

1399
01:01:04,520 --> 01:01:06,110
as well as image data.

1400
01:01:06,110 --> 01:01:08,660
We present some of these
interventional experiments

1401
01:01:08,660 --> 01:01:11,030
to show that they can solve
complex reasoning tasks.

1402
01:01:11,030 --> 01:01:12,905
There are some more
experiments in the paper.

1403
01:01:12,905 --> 01:01:15,740
I'd say that the interesting
type of future work

1404
01:01:15,740 --> 01:01:18,140
is scaling type things.

1405
01:01:18,140 --> 01:01:20,990
So we can-- not having this
mini batching approximation.

1406
01:01:20,990 --> 01:01:22,850
And then, also just
trying to expand this

1407
01:01:22,850 --> 01:01:24,540
to some more interesting
application demands.

1408
01:01:24,540 --> 01:01:25,730
So we talked a little
bit about meta learning,

1409
01:01:25,730 --> 01:01:28,400
but it could also be things
like few shot generalization

1410
01:01:28,400 --> 01:01:31,120
in general, domain adaptation,
semi-supervised learning, et

1411
01:01:31,120 --> 01:01:33,430
cetera.

1412
01:01:33,430 --> 01:01:36,090
So I think if there's
some more questions, maybe

1413
01:01:36,090 --> 01:01:38,980
we can do some more discussion.

1414
01:01:38,980 --> 01:01:40,370
Yeah, sounds good.

1415
01:01:40,370 --> 01:01:40,870
Great.

1416
01:01:40,870 --> 01:01:41,620
Thanks for the talk.

1417
01:01:41,620 --> 01:01:42,340
I think everyone had a fun time.

1418
01:01:42,340 --> 01:01:42,840
So good.

1419
01:01:42,840 --> 01:01:45,430
[CLAPPING]

1420
01:01:45,430 --> 01:01:47,290
I will just also ask
some general questions.

1421
01:01:47,290 --> 01:01:49,600
And then, we can have
a discussion session

1422
01:01:49,600 --> 01:01:52,220
with everyone after that.

1423
01:01:52,220 --> 01:01:55,240
So I think one thing
that I noticed is this--

1424
01:01:55,240 --> 01:01:57,280
you said this is
similar to k-NNs.

1425
01:01:57,280 --> 01:01:59,740
And I thought this seems
similar to like graph neural

1426
01:01:59,740 --> 01:02:02,638
networks where you can think
each data point is like a node.

1427
01:02:02,638 --> 01:02:04,180
And then, you can
think of everything

1428
01:02:04,180 --> 01:02:05,305
as a fully connected graph.

1429
01:02:05,305 --> 01:02:08,055
And you're learning some sort
of attention rate in this graph.

1430
01:02:08,055 --> 01:02:09,430
So this is like
a node prediction

1431
01:02:09,430 --> 01:02:12,980
task you are kind of doing
on this graph structure.

1432
01:02:12,980 --> 01:02:14,440
So any comment on that?

1433
01:02:14,440 --> 01:02:16,330
Is it similar to
graph neural networks

1434
01:02:16,330 --> 01:02:19,210
or is it like other differences?

1435
01:02:19,210 --> 01:02:21,550
Yeah, that's a very
good observation.

1436
01:02:21,550 --> 01:02:23,920
Yeah, I think there are a
lot of similarities to work

1437
01:02:23,920 --> 01:02:26,050
done on graph neural networks.

1438
01:02:26,050 --> 01:02:27,730
If we want to talk
about differences,

1439
01:02:27,730 --> 01:02:29,470
the differences
might be that we're

1440
01:02:29,470 --> 01:02:33,170
kind of assuming a fully
connected graph, right?

1441
01:02:33,170 --> 01:02:34,900
And so, you could
maybe also phrase

1442
01:02:34,900 --> 01:02:37,540
that as we're discovering
the relational structure.

1443
01:02:37,540 --> 01:02:40,630
Whereas, graph neural networks
usually assume that it's given.

1444
01:02:40,630 --> 01:02:42,040
But that's also not always true.

1445
01:02:42,040 --> 01:02:44,460
And so, there are a
lot of similarities.

1446
01:02:44,460 --> 01:02:46,960
I don't know, Neil, if there
is something specific you would

1447
01:02:46,960 --> 01:02:48,785
like to mention, go ahead.

1448
01:02:48,785 --> 01:02:50,160
But it's a very
good observation.

1449
01:02:50,160 --> 01:02:52,540
And we also do feel
that that's the case.

1450
01:02:52,540 --> 01:02:55,070
And we've added an extra
section on related work

1451
01:02:55,070 --> 01:02:58,180
on geographical networks in the
updated version of the paper

1452
01:02:58,180 --> 01:03:00,640
that will be online soon.

1453
01:03:00,640 --> 01:03:02,110
Got it.

1454
01:03:02,110 --> 01:03:04,570
Yeah, I agree with
everything you've said.

1455
01:03:04,570 --> 01:03:07,588
I think that the closest work
from the GNN literature that we

1456
01:03:07,588 --> 01:03:09,880
were looking at a little bit
was this neural relational

1457
01:03:09,880 --> 01:03:14,740
inference paper, which uses
message passing neural networks

1458
01:03:14,740 --> 01:03:18,310
to try to learn edges that
may or may not exist and help

1459
01:03:18,310 --> 01:03:22,030
for extrapolating,
I think, positions

1460
01:03:22,030 --> 01:03:25,540
of particles in a multi-particle
system or something, which

1461
01:03:25,540 --> 01:03:27,550
is a similar idea to us.

1462
01:03:27,550 --> 01:03:29,512
If you don't have
these edges as given,

1463
01:03:29,512 --> 01:03:30,970
the attention
mechanism can kind of

1464
01:03:30,970 --> 01:03:32,595
approximate an
interesting relationship

1465
01:03:32,595 --> 01:03:35,860
amongst some interacting things.

1466
01:03:35,860 --> 01:03:36,610
I see.

1467
01:03:36,610 --> 01:03:37,570
Got it.

1468
01:03:37,570 --> 01:03:39,230
Yeah, that's really cool.

1469
01:03:39,230 --> 01:03:41,620
Another thing is-- so you
mostly look on tabular data.

1470
01:03:41,620 --> 01:03:43,510
But can you also
have other modalities

1471
01:03:43,510 --> 01:03:45,730
like if you want to do
language or something?

1472
01:03:45,730 --> 01:03:50,640
Can you still use
non-parametric transformers?

1473
01:03:50,640 --> 01:03:54,060
Yeah, so I think part of our
motivation for doing tabular

1474
01:03:54,060 --> 01:03:56,850
was because we felt like
tabular data is, in a sense,

1475
01:03:56,850 --> 01:04:00,660
a generalization of, let's say,
the language data, for example.

1476
01:04:00,660 --> 01:04:03,990
I mean, I guess there's these
other notions that people

1477
01:04:03,990 --> 01:04:05,982
have brought up like padding.

1478
01:04:05,982 --> 01:04:07,440
But ultimately,
you can think of it

1479
01:04:07,440 --> 01:04:10,830
as a bunch of
categorical attributes.

1480
01:04:10,830 --> 01:04:13,290
So it is definitely
generalizable to things

1481
01:04:13,290 --> 01:04:14,400
like sentences.

1482
01:04:14,400 --> 01:04:16,620
And we do images.

1483
01:04:16,620 --> 01:04:19,260
So yeah.

1484
01:04:19,260 --> 01:04:21,850
I think, actually, like--

1485
01:04:21,850 --> 01:04:24,420
I always go back and
forth on whether or not

1486
01:04:24,420 --> 01:04:27,668
I think smaller or larger data
is more interesting for us.

1487
01:04:27,668 --> 01:04:29,460
So I think small data
is really interesting

1488
01:04:29,460 --> 01:04:31,890
because we can just fit the
entire data set into it.

1489
01:04:31,890 --> 01:04:35,460
And all of this just
works out of the box

1490
01:04:35,460 --> 01:04:36,750
without any extra thought.

1491
01:04:36,750 --> 01:04:39,540
But large data is actually
also really interesting

1492
01:04:39,540 --> 01:04:42,780
because, sure, you
might have to introduce

1493
01:04:42,780 --> 01:04:45,600
some approximative mechanism
or some lookup mechanism

1494
01:04:45,600 --> 01:04:49,140
because you can't always
have the entire data set in.

1495
01:04:49,140 --> 01:04:51,720
But, at the same time, you
are very explicitly kind

1496
01:04:51,720 --> 01:04:54,540
of trading off the
compute that you

1497
01:04:54,540 --> 01:04:58,620
use to look up with the
compute that you need to store.

1498
01:04:58,620 --> 01:05:03,358
How many parameters in GPT
are used for storing data?

1499
01:05:03,358 --> 01:05:05,650
There's lots of memorization
happening in these models,

1500
01:05:05,650 --> 01:05:06,520
and we know that.

1501
01:05:06,520 --> 01:05:10,080
And so, maybe we can use the
parameters more efficiently

1502
01:05:10,080 --> 01:05:13,050
to learn lookup
type behavior that

1503
01:05:13,050 --> 01:05:16,250
is more close to this
neural k-NN or whatever.

1504
01:05:16,250 --> 01:05:19,810
So I think these are
very exciting questions.

1505
01:05:19,810 --> 01:05:20,760
Yeah.

1506
